[{"id":"VRCE-2-12-1","kanda":2,"kandaId":"ayodhya","sarga":12,"verse":1,"english":"Then that sinful woman, seeing the descendant of Ikşvāku distressed on account of his son, and deprived of consciousness, and lying inert on the ground, spoke to him, saying.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.647,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-2-14-1"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"2012001","sanskritFile":"Ram02.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"dd0d0a0fd90f033dbf9bbeadc8fd7a89a42952e86c418ad622dc0987814ffd06","compact":"537e0e9f55160353fa951b135c067640f9a27a8bbf0be3e9a701e0efcbaea1c3","nasal":"537e0e9f55160353fa951b135c067640f9a27a8bbf0be3e9a701e0efcbaea1c3"}},{"id":"VRCE-2-12-2","kanda":2,"kandaId":"ayodhya","sarga":12,"verse":2,"english":"Having promised to grant me the boon, how distressed do you lie on the ground, as if you did commit some sin? It behove you to keep untainted your dignity by performing what you have promised.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":1.0,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-2-14-2"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"2012002","sanskritFile":"Ram02.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"fb3685553820405e7fee4e5585271ab446d1dfd88b0a303724d3ad574432e51d","compact":"466eeca779551b40fffa1a82c750c088729f1c2d4e211856fa48c1d75b6236f0","nasal":"b754b4442dd8c05ff5fb8190724b2701730c0e85b451075357b469b4e1051aa4"}},{"id":"VRCE-2-12-3","kanda":2,"kandaId":"ayodhya","sarga":12,"verse":3,"english":"Truth, say persons cognizant of it, is the prime virtue. And it is in the interests of virtue, that I have been exhorting you.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.613,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-2-14-3"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"2012003","sanskritFile":"Ram02.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"d8e21ea160b188847a976d2ff953ac77150f85424b274501025881a28f80afaa","compact":"b73e15d0a7af92ae48e0c034e24b94f32f954cdefc732210402601ab6e91e519","nasal":"b73e15d0a7af92ae48e0c034e24b94f32f954cdefc732210402601ab6e91e519"}},{"id":"VRCE-2-12-4","kanda":2,"kandaId":"ayodhya","sarga":12,"verse":4,"english":"Having promised his own person to the hawk, that lord of earth, Saivya, having granted the same to the bird, went the excellent way.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.803,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-2-14-4"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"2012004","sanskritFile":"Ram02.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"07da80df341f2bc8f35291ff8b79cbea7bfee1adcb386f11712e2b8425c3013b","compact":"fd75d017d717342d94f90e294e74e5a81864295f5808d1f6689c56c0ad7ee4c0","nasal":"6a088c6b12dd2ca6050012c2b12d49d9ab71307f26ad21a881a613cf0f4a2163"}},{"id":"VRCE-2-12-5","kanda":2,"kandaId":"ayodhya","sarga":12,"verse":5,"english":"In the same manner, Alarka, being asked, plucked out his eyes readily and bestowed them on a certain Brāhmana versed in the Vedas.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.921,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-2-14-5"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"2012005","sanskritFile":"Ram02.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"e85dd997614fde9e7d9a1a5f2495f6532f6e5d713daaa28fc49b4490f5d5b011","compact":"243ec194410ce6d276bdaf66ed08de5f80b19596e40bde51c4e3f0f09b00c97b","nasal":"243ec194410ce6d276bdaf66ed08de5f80b19596e40bde51c4e3f0f09b00c97b"}},{"id":"VRCE-2-12-6","kanda":2,"kandaId":"ayodhya","sarga":12,"verse":6,"english":"And the lord of rivers, having promised, even on occasions of Parvas, does not pass over his bounds for the sake of truth.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.881,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-2-14-6"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"2012006","sanskritFile":"Ram02.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"822dc340794a62ee9cbcd9f2003d781689c83ac483d186db3a3eeb8f28ce6552","compact":"53bf0e27db98b50e41ca2c9b5a3bed9dfab9c55cbe2d401d437718e15af5d9d7","nasal":"53bf0e27db98b50e41ca2c9b5a3bed9dfab9c55cbe2d401d437718e15af5d9d7"}},{"id":"VRCE-2-12-7","kanda":2,"kandaId":"ayodhya","sarga":12,"verse":7,"english":"If you did not fulfil this noble vow, O worshipful Lord, you have made to me, forsaken by you will I even in your very presence renounce my life.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":1.0,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-2-14-10"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"2012007","sanskritFile":"Ram02.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"115f10e5f75979aaf3a114e463a24195affe38e14a9a23cd8573e8b11181b456","compact":"bbc97eea4f9bf0d010a4e90af3cf8d4c0a0668274309349f0fa8f93326044ded","nasal":"bbc97eea4f9bf0d010a4e90af3cf8d4c0a0668274309349f0fa8f93326044ded"}},{"id":"VRCE-2-12-8","kanda":2,"kandaId":"ayodhya","sarga":12,"verse":8,"english":"Thus fearlessly urged by Kaikeyſ the king could not take off from himself the noose (of promise), even as Bali could not take off the noose that had been fixed upon him (by Upendra) at the instance of Indra.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":1.0,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-2-14-11"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"2012008","sanskritFile":"Ram02.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"ebffe22b2e50b0f9f77b428de3ea806f757692da87b8c5fea93e003b17b661af","compact":"b5084d93f2b39a291e0dcf1e90c586af3b8d8b47bd8764f159edb8ab9704500f","nasal":"5dee55d7eae031fedae76b008724673d0884e72e291ac0508629bc8b3ff43425"}},{"id":"VRCE-2-12-9","kanda":2,"kandaId":"ayodhya","sarga":12,"verse":9,"english":"Thereupon, the king looked blank, and his heart became agitated, like to a beast of burden moving tortuously when placed within the yoke and wheels.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.857,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-2-14-12"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"2012009","sanskritFile":"Ram02.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"e2ad9bd89ec47b19139f84939700a25c23488123afb17aaee980c08886526fee","compact":"030f78697ed04910f4d7fed2718b68195621fe0972ed781c66e26116e6538fe1","nasal":"cc73ebd19e2b0fcb52014d7a76973d708187934581cd6ae9e6af59ee30bb1890"}},{"id":"VRCE-2-12-10","kanda":2,"kandaId":"ayodhya","sarga":12,"verse":10,"english":"Then calming himself with a great effort, the king, as if not seeing Kaikeyi, with his haggard eyes, addressed her, saying.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.716,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-2-14-13"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"2012010","sanskritFile":"Ram02.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"559680e58816c9b664ded6f758c02b9f1bb6b82e5b4a2ab582b23f3f7b9b9f45","compact":"d3f12c89cb01afdea9c28ed1eae53d46766f931b7125bca82cdcb6965cbb1760","nasal":"ff6594ee62444a82b8018fc0b7110c1046c2bf46723e6352fcd81b7c995fc001"}},{"id":"VRCE-2-12-11","kanda":2,"kandaId":"ayodhya","sarga":12,"verse":11,"english":"\"I do here, O wicked wretch, renounce that hand of yours which I had held with mantras before the sacrificial fire, and I do also renounce along with you own-begotten* your son Bharata. * This is a new coinage rendered necessary for the exigencies of translation. If 'first-born' is justifiable, 'own-begotten,' is also such.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.733,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-2-14-14"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"2012011","sanskritFile":"Ram02.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"1a790eb93a9729e5147ec64801f37491086ef9adf56d883d798a2eef5f83cfda","compact":"4731f5d1fa0de7dbf4de586a0353912cb7733b5bfa96338b91dd2332ca4bf1e1","nasal":"3d1fe0eb90e9844e49b9a36d5bd837c55c06b04c8bf6be125ffd7fe18cc4f156"}},{"id":"VRCE-2-12-12","kanda":2,"kandaId":"ayodhya","sarga":12,"verse":12,"english":"Then Kaikeyi of vile ways versed in speech, rendered senseless by wrath, again addressed the monarch in harsh language, saying.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":1.0,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-2-14-19"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"2012012","sanskritFile":"Ram02.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"adaf643b2563c580628d02f4d5af98f68d9be0c55342f86f5d72140412c43f16","compact":"b7f4e54b91a2049bac107755dd0be4054b422ea9f14cd8142ce165ae9d162113","nasal":"b7f4e54b91a2049bac107755dd0be4054b422ea9f14cd8142ce165ae9d162113"}},{"id":"VRCE-2-12-13","kanda":2,"kandaId":"ayodhya","sarga":12,"verse":13,"english":"What words, O king, do you say, comparable to poison or painful indisposition's. It behove you to summon hither your son, the energetic Rāma. Having established my son in the kingdom, and rendered Rāma a ranger of the woods and made myself rid of rivals, I shall attain my end.","alignment":{"type":"grouped","confidence":1.0,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-2-14-20"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"2012013","sanskritFile":"Ram02.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"bfee23c67f666f2fc434f2acf8a2f5590c8f4fa38f4c4abb85d7de6d7cfb7339","compact":"63c44170bd077c169c5440f1a632012005cefabfbad52349c84ceed18a46fc67","nasal":"63c44170bd077c169c5440f1a632012005cefabfbad52349c84ceed18a46fc67"}},{"id":"VRCE-2-12-14","kanda":2,"kandaId":"ayodhya","sarga":12,"verse":14,"english":"What words, O king, do you say, comparable to poison or painful indisposition's. It behove you to summon hither your son, the energetic Rāma. Having established my son in the kingdom, and rendered Rāma a ranger of the woods and made myself rid of rivals, I shall attain my end.","alignment":{"type":"grouped","confidence":1.0,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-2-14-20"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"2012014","sanskritFile":"Ram02.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"7d87cac2b3633492f156de501e02e3df467ac21de138b84480739e2b67717593","compact":"9084d0db1a2933adbe4dd5c1cb759a6b0ddd01c0706d9dfe000ab53cee11a3cc","nasal":"9084d0db1a2933adbe4dd5c1cb759a6b0ddd01c0706d9dfe000ab53cee11a3cc"}},{"id":"VRCE-2-12-15","kanda":2,"kandaId":"ayodhya","sarga":12,"verse":15,"english":"Thus urged by Kaikeyī, the king like to an excellent steed stung by a sharp goad, again spoke to her.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.484,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-2-14-21"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"2012015","sanskritFile":"Ram02.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"0bae7876b03ae9fd7e6796be69031730a25bf4fb924d54096d003303d6470d41","compact":"b7cd6963b9bc7c6b79844b3a05caf49799afae8744d8fc1698314b050367ed0a","nasal":"d2f37586dfbbc9cc8726188fd916fb961d45782acea791eafc46a44e484b7033"}},{"id":"VRCE-2-12-16","kanda":2,"kandaId":"ayodhya","sarga":12,"verse":16,"english":"I have been bound fast by the ties of virtue, therefore have I lost my senses. I now only wish to behold my beloved eldest son, the righteous Rama.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":1.0,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-2-14-22"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"2012016","sanskritFile":"Ram02.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"b1ef8f7293a104ef2ddf4140b0c40bfd7dd3850ceb2eec2d815681a556507836","compact":"3e21fa552af2a3a79ec0516a8dd6dae3ab68c2a573af30aaa20c33bb916a6710","nasal":"806959dbc80aa1e3616e1070d8d3105a1fb1cf3a95a6b228341ca17ffee3baee"}},{"id":"VRCE-2-12-17","kanda":2,"kandaId":"ayodhya","sarga":12,"verse":17,"english":"The hump-backed Mantharā, skilled in speech, who really sought Kaikeyi's welfare, hearing the latter's sweet-accented words, displaying sorrow greater than Kaikeyi's own, lamenting, and enlisting Kaikeyi's feelings against Rāma, uttered words inflamed with anger, saying.","alignment":{"type":"approximate","confidence":0.145,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-2-7-14"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"2012017","sanskritFile":"Ram02.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"1be661ab3f19f3c3f483bebd5f6942719cb87f64bb46f7a7e479f8ad197f8f5d","compact":"b18a26aafc5ddff4326a09a5af498cc0b6f9a13ce3b7f39c86056ccd0c1a7602","nasal":"a40b6cf22df65ee58e1fe4f1cedc02fcb789353babea109ed7c8d6e6cf46fd34"}},{"id":"VRCE-2-12-18","kanda":2,"kandaId":"ayodhya","sarga":12,"verse":18,"english":"Then the graceful and virtuous king, waxing disconsolate, on account of his son, and with his eyes reddened with the effect of grief, seeing the charioteer, said, “This eulogy of your pains me the more.\"","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.824,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-2-14-41"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"2012018","sanskritFile":"Ram02.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"338cb296a60e954ea21b33b9a7450da6471d0416d1ec9183913f14ac87e6bcba","compact":"f9a25699da26c8f8f6b80174da57d2dcd29b37f1cdcf3332a6a54e36880504b5","nasal":"489e0ef59183ac65ef8ef5ce67ec93f02b49c3007bb253746b465c1019ee96e7"}},{"id":"VRCE-2-12-19","kanda":2,"kandaId":"ayodhya","sarga":12,"verse":19,"english":"Hearing those sorrowful words and finding the lord of earth aggrieved thus, Sumantra with clasped hands went off a little.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.975,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-2-14-42"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"2012019","sanskritFile":"Ram02.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"458dd471eedc97687affcd25a13c8b642ea5573106604f3ddb8633d9d2e7d6e4","compact":"5fcd15362e5ed97e477053e3e857470632148a19b843e4e98a7e8a0037db4c53","nasal":"d93d1ae4da316a71a3e7fa6344e4f0734a12835971e70a2a1d69d44af22d51e0"}},{"id":"VRCE-2-12-20","kanda":2,"kandaId":"ayodhya","sarga":12,"verse":20,"english":"Seeing the king utterly incapable of speaking any thing personally owing to his heavy sorrow, Kaikeyī, the best counsellor in matters like these, addressed Sumantra with the following words.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":1.0,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-2-14-43"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"2012020","sanskritFile":"Ram02.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"784d11107ba4128c1b0e348d52b214a66325a58d1decd960cd330a5c7d84835c","compact":"b0272c5d08b41e0c2a5cc09cc9ca84da73b82e3cdc96149e5863dd01c9e1848a","nasal":"9e64bdedfa00525bd97a6f9718531d9c3a3e016d903bf776b09f7751107ebfe1"}},{"id":"VRCE-2-12-21","kanda":2,"kandaId":"ayodhya","sarga":12,"verse":21,"english":"Sumantra, Bring the beautiful Răma here, I want to see him. Thinking that some good would accrue to Rāma, he was pleased at heart and away he went speedily delighted at the royal mandate. Being asked again by Kaikeyi to hurry on at the time of departure Sumantra thought within himself.","alignment":{"type":"grouped","confidence":1.0,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-2-14-47"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"2012021","sanskritFile":"Ram02.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"59ed904a61d56b6a05a7d84122c7a85d180568ba07f80b14d8ed3e1b1232d365","compact":"fd4f2b5bf9d596754b1b00792cf03355b37d6e5269ec70cc7c70961bdbf2788c","nasal":"29be08628670f2ee983666e3c95a8b217a9b590df0a34f1e5f9228d85f57704d"}},{"id":"VRCE-2-12-22","kanda":2,"kandaId":"ayodhya","sarga":12,"verse":22,"english":"Sumantra, Bring the beautiful Răma here, I want to see him. Thinking that some good would accrue to Rāma, he was pleased at heart and away he went speedily delighted at the royal mandate. Being asked again by Kaikeyi to hurry on at the time of departure Sumantra thought within himself.","alignment":{"type":"grouped","confidence":0.449,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-2-14-47"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"2012022","sanskritFile":"Ram02.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"88f0709494656208f84cf99a6360f7581f3d6745b971651098203f017618ce18","compact":"047405fd28a67acd92da478111308a5cb11982de8fc6cb82c03d480d412cd318","nasal":"7550db8823cb447c8458e70cda5443b4d943d762a21908abcf61f8112627896e"}},{"id":"VRCE-2-12-23","kanda":2,"kandaId":"ayodhya","sarga":12,"verse":23,"english":"\"Evident it is that the Queen has become impatient to see Rāma's installation and hence is the hurry, and the king will now take rest.' Thinking this the energetic charioteer with great delight, intent on seeing Rāma, issued out of the city like a lake near the ocean.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.709,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-2-14-48"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"2012023","sanskritFile":"Ram02.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"d6a5f30e28b7d45aedc33abb4afde01b17ba0df13ade46cbe52e19b6059af6ef","compact":"5e7a3a59063bff996da01832fe89239a0ffe6528e54e7fbeb3f5056bef88f1f9","nasal":"5e7a3a59063bff996da01832fe89239a0ffe6528e54e7fbeb3f5056bef88f1f9"}},{"id":"VRCE-2-12-24","kanda":2,"kandaId":"ayodhya","sarga":12,"verse":24,"english":"Having come out of the presence of the king suddenly, he saw the warders, various citizens and great personages sitting at the gate.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.451,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-2-14-49"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"2012024","sanskritFile":"Ram02.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"5f60f8cb25513ef7c1b3075960415cb2f7f6442e7b6f0ec82cf9521ccbfcf912","compact":"8f5a63f8325c5867f0995b0aea2267bfee770fa1da1df704ad95616aa73a7778","nasal":"b218f06fb3b3e8fc59ebe0b5275975ee996458324d7a5aae9b1aa4d2b351900d"}}]
